the ancient philosophers Aristotle  Diogenes Cicero Seneca and many others  all of them are fairly well known for  their works but as fancy is all of that  philosophizing was how did they actually  earn a living  how did an ancient philosopher pay the  bills well there are many answers and  circumstances were often individual but  there were several common ways that  philosophers made their living and  shockingly they were largely determined  by the philosophers own beliefs  the first way that philosophers could  make a living was by giving lessons and  instructions it wasn't just philosophy  that would be taught though rhetoric and  other topics like mathematics were also  included the hope or at least the sales  pitch was that philosophers would teach  their students to be virtuous competent  leaders who would run their family's  estates competently this way of earning  a living was often criticized by other  philosophers in selling knowledge was  seen as immoral the second way was  simple they'd beg you see many  philosophers weren't very wealthy would  often take to the streets begging for  food or money the most famous example of  this is of course Diogenes who was  homeless and even briefly lived in a jar  and he would often berate people on the  streets until they paid him he and other  philosophers were often given  accommodation and food in the houses of  wealthy friends who wanted to hear what  they had to say and the third way they  made money was perhaps the most common  they'd find a wealthy patron now this  could take several forms sometimes a  wealthy benefactor would leave a very  large sum of money to a philosopher they  like the work of another way is  demonstrated by the philosopher  Epictetus  he wrote at the time he approached a  potential patron and goaded him into  becoming his supporter he asked why he  would entrust his body to a doctor his  horses to an avenue but not his soul and  virtuous well-being to a philosopher a  fair point perhaps but it ended with  epictetus getting punched in the face  and no patron he became his teacher  instead way number four was to become an  on hand philosopher Aristotle for  instance was a personal advisor to  Alexander the Great who had be kept  uncool for whenever the King had a  question and Seneca acted as a political  advisor to emperor Nero these were often  very lucrative positions but were also  quite dangerous since monarchs weren't  the most easy of patrons and the fifth  way was that they already had jobs and  would philosophize on the side Cicero  for example was a lawyer a Roman senator  and a consul and his philosophy was  often tied to public service whereas  Marcus Aurelius made his living by you  know being the Emperor  so to summarize it shouldn't surprise  you that in the days of subsistence  farming thinking for a living wasn't  really a viable well-paid career for  most it  largely down to the patronage of the  wealthy that ancient philosophers could  spend as much time philosophizing as  they did so how did ancient philosophers  make their money some had jobs some  begged for scraps and many were kept by  the wealthy I hope you enjoyed this  episode and a special thanks to my  patrons James Bissonnette the pastry  section Danny Maloney Marvin Castle  Kelly moneymaker Rob Waterhouse John  bisko's moe James Castaneda Aaron the  whites Jordan long way Marcus Arsena  Gustav Swan Jerry Lambton John Bailey  calling Castleman Rashid Ali spinning  three plates filled oink oink  Maggie Pats Kowski David Silverman Izzy  Lexi Schwinn Robert Wetzel Skye  Chappelle Spencer Lightfoot and Winston  K word
 

